Solute: The substance that is being dissolved (usually present in a smaller amount).

Solvent: The substance doing the dissolving (usually present in a larger amount). Water is the most common solvent and is often called the "universal solvent."

Solution: The uniform, homogenous mixture formed when the solute is completely dissolved in the solvent.
Solute + Solvent = Solution
Real-World Example
If you mix a spoonful of sugar into a glass of water until it completely disappears:

Solute: Sugar

Solvent: Water

Solution: Sugar water
M = {moles of solute}}\{liters of solution}
m ={moles of solute}\{kilograms of solvent}
